IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cons54-nouns`

-- Row il-54. THE CITATIONS, as typed values.
-- A citation is encoded as TWO descriptions: what the citing words are about, and what the words they use find at the other end. A test compares them. Where they differ, the citation does
-- not find what it is about; that is a defect, kept in an expected-red file with its count declared in check.sh and encoding.json.
-- Six citations are recorded. Three are defects: section 31א(א)(7) cites "סעיף 18א(5)" and section 31א(א)(8) cites "סעיף 18א(1)", each with no letter for the subsection;
-- and the Ninth Schedule, item 6, uses the short name "חוק התקשורת", which section 105 of the Communications (Broadcasts) Law, 5786-2026, takes out of the Second and Third Schedules and not out of the Ninth.
-- Three are sound and are here as controls: section 31א(א)(7)'s own citation of "סעיף 18א(א1)", section 31א(א)(5)'s citation of "סעיף 17ב(ד)", and the Third Schedule's use of the same short name,
-- which section 105(2) of the same Law rewrites together with the definition.
-- LIMBS. Limb (a) is the question whether the target is as the citing words say; limb (b) is the question whether the citing words still read so in the latest official text of the citing provision.
-- Each limb says where it was read from (see `Where a reading comes from` in cons54-nouns.l4). The Hebrew Wikisource text is not official.
-- DAYS. Three days are computed by this row from a count of months or years from a day of publication (the stopping days of the first two defects and of the third, and the starting days of the Ninth Schedule).
-- Whether the day of publication counts, and whether the months are Gregorian, was not checked against the Interpretation Law (fork D2 in NOTES.md), so the tests ask only about days well clear of each of them.
-- A date for which the sources held give no answer is NOTHING; there is no REFUSE.
-- Nothing after Sefer HaChukim 2310 (11.8.2011) was read for the words of section 31א(א)(7) and (8), and which instrument wrote paragraphs (1) and (5) of section 18א(א) was not read.

§ `Cross-references`

GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the citation points at what it says` MEANS c's `the citing words say it points at` EQUALS c's `the target is`

GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`both limbs were read from the official instruments` MEANS
    c's `limb (a) was read from` EQUALS `the official instrument, read from its page image`
    AND c's `limb (b) was read from` EQUALS `the official instrument, read from its page image`

-- Whether the words the citing provision uses still reach their text on a day. The only answer the sources held give is the day they stop doing so and the days from then on;
-- for any earlier day, and for a citation with no recorded stopping day, the answer is NOTHING, which is not a statement that they do (fork D1 in NOTES.md).
GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
      day IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the words still reach their text on` MEANS
    CONSIDER c's `the words stop reaching their text on`
        WHEN JUST stopped THEN IF day AT LEAST stopped THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- The documented fork of context: whether the citing words, read with the words around them, reach what they are about. NOTHING where this row does not say.
GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the citation reaches what the citing words are about once read in context` MEANS
    CONSIDER c's `read in context the citing words reach`
        WHEN JUST reached THEN JUST (reached EQUALS c's `the citing words say it points at`)
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- Whether the citing words are in force, even after every postponement the instrument allows, no later than the day the words stop reaching their text.
GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the citing words are in force, even after every postponement, no later than the day their words stop reaching their text` MEANS
    CONSIDER c's `the citing words take effect no later than if every postponement is used`
        WHEN JUST latest THEN
            CONSIDER c's `the words stop reaching their text on`
                WHEN JUST stopped THEN IF stopped AT LEAST latest THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
                WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

